    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4311995d-eed0-5066-ac9e-9398e920a584",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨- 3 t - 2,3 t - 1⟩$ at the point $t=1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} + y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} + y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 2 x,2 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=- 3 t - 2$, $y=3 t - 1$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨- 2 \\left(- 3 t - 2\\right),2 \\left(3 t - 1\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6 t + 4,6 t - 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 t - 2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t - 1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨6 t + 4,6 t - 2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(6 t + 4\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(6 t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(1))] & =-18 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
