    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4d82c4a3-5307-584f-9322-f503a600795a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} - y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨- 3 t - 1,2 t + 2⟩$ at the point $t=-2.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} - y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} - y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6 x,- 2 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=- 3 t - 1$, $y=2 t + 2$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨6 \\left(- 3 t - 1\\right),- 2 \\left(2 t + 2\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 18 t - 6,- 4 t - 4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 t - 1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨- 18 t - 6,- 4 t - 4⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(- 18 t - 6\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(- 4 t - 4\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\  & =46 t + 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(-2))] & =46 (-2) + 10 \\\\  & =-82.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
